private function productionPrompt(array $developed,string $shop):string{$spec=wp_json_encode($developed['spec']??[],JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES|JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE);return"Create a production-ready DigiForge asset manifest for a {$shop} Etsy product. Return compact JSON only with product_assets and marketing_assets arrays. Keep the complete response comfortably below the provider output limit: generate the minimum sellable asset set, avoid duplicate content across files, and keep each asset concise. Each asset needs unique asset_key, filename, format (html/txt/json/csv/svg/pdf), purpose and complete content. Product assets are customer-delivery files; marketing assets are listing-only. The approved product specification is the binding production contract: {$spec}. Do not silently substitute a different deliverable type, editing method, feature set, variant structure, or customer promise. Every HTML asset must be a complete document with <!doctype html>, html, head, style and body. Never emit INSERT, TODO, example.com, placeholder URLs, fake QR destinations or fake Canva links. External-service deliverables such as Canva templates may be claimed only when the specification contains a verified usable URL; otherwise the manifest and ALL marketing copy must explicitly exclude that unavailable capability rather than claim it. PDF deliverables must be emitted as format pdf with complete text/page content so DigiForge generates a real local PDF; never substitute HTML for an approved PDF. QR, map, RSVP, registry, hotel, or other link functionality may be claimed only when a real approved destination is available; otherwise omit both the functionality and every marketing claim for it. Preserve the approved language editions and variant structure exactly unless an unavailable external dependency makes a specific capability impossible, in which case remove the same claim consistently from product content, instructions, manifests, previews and listing copy. Before returning JSON, cross-check every customer-facing claim against an actual product asset in this manifest. Marketing must describe only what the generated package really contains. Filenames are flat local filenames: never require or advertise nested directory paths, and use the exact same flat filename in the specification-facing manifest and marketing evidence. Every promised language edition must contain complete translated customer-facing prose, not untranslated tokens or heading-only translations. Optional link, QR, map, RSVP, registry, hotel or transport fields must be omitted everywhere unless a verified destination exists; do not promise blank link fields that are not generated. For provenance, include a customer product asset named LICENSE-AND-PROVENANCE.txt that truthfully records DigiForge generation, creation context, locally generated text/layout/SVG status, system-font-only status when true, and that no third-party artwork is bundled; do not invent third-party licenses or rights evidence. Avoid unnecessary third-party brand names in customer assets and marketing copy. The customer package ZIP must contain product assets only and must exclude all marketing assets; the delivery manifest must explicitly enumerate the exact customer filenames and state marketing_assets_included=false. Marketing assets must not be passed into customer package construction. Do not invent licensing evidence; prefer locally generated text/layout/SVG content. Make all content final, commercially usable and internally consistent. Do not call external services, publish anything, or include scripts/iframes/remote resources.";}
private function error(string $code,string $message,int $status=400):WP_Error{return new WP_Error('digiforge_'.$code,$message,['status'=>$status]);}
